Building a mobile projection mapping app requires balancing a highly responsive touch interface with raw GPU rendering power. Because smartphones have limited thermal ceilings compared to desktops, the architecture must be incredibly efficient.A highly effective approach is to separate the user interface from the rendering engine. You can build the control dashboard using React and Tailwind CSS to create a clean, modular layout. For the mapping mechanics—where users drag corner pins, draw masks, or adjust bezier curves—overlaying interactive SVG animations provides a fluid, mathematically precise touch experience without bogging down the main render thread.Here is how to structure the core components of the app:1. The Rendering Engine (WebGL)The actual video processing must happen directly on the smartphone's GPU. You cannot manipulate 1080p or 4K video frames on the CPU without massive frame drops.The Setup: Use an HTML5 <canvas> element driven by WebGL (or a wrapper like Three.js).Video as a Texture: The app decodes the video file and passes the raw frames into GPU memory as a 2D texture.The Mesh Layer: You generate a 2D geometric mesh (a grid of triangles) on the canvas. The video texture is mapped onto this mesh.2. The Touch-to-Vertex BridgeWhen a user drags a node in your React/SVG interface to warp the image around a physical object, the app must translate those 2D screen coordinates into the WebGL environment.The UI tracks the X/Y coordinates of the user's finger.Those coordinates are instantly passed to the WebGL Vertex Shader.The shader recalculates the position of the mesh in real-time, pulling the video texture along with it, creating the warped perspective.3. The Backend and State ManagementDepending on how you want the app to function, you have two distinct architecture paths for handling the data:Standalone Mobile App: If the phone is doing the projecting (via a USB-C to HDMI cable), all state management happens locally. The React frontend passes data directly to the WebGL canvas, and projects are saved in the device's local storage.Mobile Controller for Hardware: If the app is designed to control a dedicated projection box (like a Raspberry Pi cluster hooked up to the projector), you can wrap the rendering engine in a Docker container on the hardware. The smartphone app then communicates over Wi-Fi, hitting Flask API endpoints on the Pi to send the SVG mesh coordinates, change media assets, and update the project state.The Real-Time PipelineOnce the app is running, every single frame of video goes through this strict sequence to ensure low-latency output:1.Hardware Decoding:Bypass the CPU.The smartphone's hardware decoder reads the video file and outputs raw visual data directly to the GPU's Video RAM.2.Texture Binding:The raw frame is bound as a WebGL texture so the graphics API can manipulate it natively.3.Vertex Warping (The Mesh):Driven by the SVG UI.The Vertex Shader applies the user's custom layout—displacing the grid points based on the corner pins and curves drawn in the control dashboard.4.Fragment Processing (The Output):The Fragment Shader samples the video texture, applies any color correction, masks, or brightness tweaks, and pushes the final distorted frame to the screen (and out to the projector).